.TE
.SH CONFIGURATION RESOLUTION LIFECYCLE
.IP "1." 3
A new shell process inspects its invocation flags and execution
privileges.
If \f[CR]\-\-no\-config\f[R], \f[CR]\-p\f[R]
(\f[CR]\-\-privileged\f[R]), or a setuid/setgid context is active,
settings files and \f[CR]KOSHCONF\f[R] are skipped and removed.
See Skipping startup files for the full exclusion list.
.IP "2." 3
The shell parses the system settings file
(\f[CR]/etc/kosh/kosh.conf\f[R] or
\f[CR]%ProgramData%\[rs]kosh\[rs]kosh.conf\f[R]) if it passes ownership
and permission checks.
.IP "3." 3
The shell parses the user settings file
(\f[CR]$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/kosh/kosh.conf\f[R] or
\f[CR]\[ti]/.config/kosh/kosh.conf\f[R]), unless the POSIX effective
user is \f[CR]root\f[R].
User settings override system settings.
The mood applies first, wherever its line appears, and the other lines
are read under the resulting session mood.
.IP "4." 3
The shell decodes and applies an inherited \f[CR]KOSHCONF\f[R]
environment variable, then removes \f[CR]KOSHCONF\f[R] from the
environment before any command runs.
.IP "5." 3
The shell applies inherited \f[CR]KOSH_HISTORY_FILE\f[R] and
\f[CR]KOSH_HISTORY_SIZE\f[R] variables and explicit command\-line flags.
Command\-line flags override both settings files and
\f[CR]KOSHCONF\f[R].
.IP "6." 3
Interactive and login shells source the startup scripts selected by
\f[CR]\-L\f[R] (\f[CR]\-\-init\-moods\f[R]), \f[CR]init_moods\f[R], or
the active session mood.
Other shells source the startup files of the session mood, such as
\f[CR]ENV\f[R] or \f[CR]BASH_ENV\f[R].
.SH OVERRIDE SETTINGS WITH COMMAND\-LINE OPTIONS
An option passed on the command line (enabled or disabled) overrides
settings files and \f[CR]KOSHCONF\f[R].
Koshka\-specific flags appear first in the table below, followed by
general, POSIX, and Bash\-compatible flags.
.PP
