T{
Compatibility
T}@T{
\f[CR]\-M, \-\-mood=NAME\f[R]
T}@T{
Precedence order from highest to lowest is \f[CR]\-\-mood\f[R],
\f[CR]\-\-dumb\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-posix\f[R] / \f[CR]\-o posix\f[R],
invocation basename (\f[CR]sh\f[R], \f[CR]dash\f[R], \f[CR]bash\f[R], or
\f[CR]rbash\f[R]), script shebang, and the configured mood (default
\f[CR]kosh\f[R]).
Without \f[CR]\-\-mood\f[R], the first line of a script file operand
selects \f[CR]bash\f[R] for a Bash shebang, \f[CR]sh\f[R] for an
\f[CR]sh\f[R] or \f[CR]dash\f[R] shebang, and \f[CR]kosh\f[R] for a
\f[CR]kosh\f[R] shebang (directly or via \f[CR]env\f[R]).
Any other interpreter, or no shebang, leaves the configured mood.
Shebang inspection is skipped for \f[CR]\-c\f[R], standard input, and
\f[CR]\-\-lint\f[R] without \f[CR]\-I\f[R].
T}
T{
Compatibility
T}@T{
\f[CR]\-L, \-\-init\-moods=LIST\f[R]
T}@T{
Accepts comma\-separated mood names or repeated \f[CR]\-L\f[R] flags.
Defaults to \f[CR]init_moods\f[R] from settings for interactive or login
shells, and to the active execution mood for non\-interactive shells.
T}
T{
Compatibility
T}@T{
\f[CR]\-I, \-\-enable\-mimicry\f[R]
T}@T{
A script started as a command runs inside Koshka under the mood its
shell shebang selects, instead of under that interpreter.
T}
T{
Compatibility
T}@T{
\f[CR]\-\-dumb\f[R]
T}@T{
An explicit \f[CR]\-\-tab\-selector\f[R] flag overrides the
\f[CR]plain\f[R] selector set by \f[CR]\-\-dumb\f[R].
T}
T{
Auxiliary
T}@T{
\f[CR]\-\-format\f[R]
T}@T{
Ignores \f[CR]\-c\f[R] command strings.
Formatting more than one file operand requires \f[CR]\-\-apply\f[R].
T}
T{
Auxiliary
T}@T{
\f[CR]\-\-apply\f[R]
T}@T{
Requires \f[CR]\-\-lint\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-format\f[R], or both, plus at
least one file operand.
Rejects standard input, \f[CR]\-s\f[R], \f[CR]\-i\f[R], \f[CR]\-c\f[R],
and \f[CR]\-\f[R].
T}
T{
Auxiliary
T}@T{
\f[CR]\-\-as\-language\-server\f[R]
T}@T{
Rejects file operands, \f[CR]\-c\f[R], \f[CR]\-s\f[R], \f[CR]\-i\f[R],
\f[CR]\-\-lint\f[R], \f[CR]\-\-format\f[R], and \f[CR]\-\-apply\f[R].
T}
T{
Koshka
T}@T{
\f[CR]\-\-no\-config\f[R]
T}@T{
Leaves \f[CR]PATH\f[R] and the startup files unchanged.
T}
T{
Koshka
T}@T{
\f[CR]\-W, \-WW, \-WWW\f[R]
T}@T{
Analysis checks are grouped into \f[CR]strict\f[R], \f[CR]lenient\f[R],
and \f[CR]annoying\f[R] tiers.
In the \f[CR]kosh\f[R] mood, \f[CR]strict\f[R] and \f[CR]lenient\f[R]
checks are errors and \f[CR]annoying\f[R] checks are warnings by
default.
\f[CR]\-W\f[R] retains that baseline, \f[CR]\-WW\f[R] demotes
\f[CR]lenient\f[R] errors to warnings, and \f[CR]\-WWW\f[R] demotes both
\f[CR]lenient\f[R] and \f[CR]strict\f[R] errors to warnings.
In compatibility moods (\f[CR]bash\f[R], \f[CR]sh\f[R],
\f[CR]bash\-posix\f[R]), static analysis is off by default;
\f[CR]\-W\f[R] enables \f[CR]strict\f[R] warnings, \f[CR]\-WW\f[R]
enables \f[CR]strict\f[R] and \f[CR]lenient\f[R] warnings, and
\f[CR]\-WWW\f[R] enables all three tiers as warnings.
Levels set via \f[CR]set\f[R] propagate to child shells through
\f[CR]KOSH_ANALYSIS\f[R].
Runtime diagnostics are unaffected by \f[CR]\-W\f[R], except that
compatibility moods warn on empty unset\-variable expansion only when a
warning level is active.
T}
T{
Koshka
T}@T{
\f[CR]\-\-tab\-selector MODE\f[R]
T}@T{
Accepts \f[CR]interactive\f[R] (default), \f[CR]external\f[R], or
\f[CR]plain\f[R].
See Line editing.
T}
T{
Koshka
T}@T{
\f[CR]\-\-enable\-koshkit\f[R]
T}@T{
Defaults to \f[CR]off\f[R].
Affects only compatibility moods because the \f[CR]kosh\f[R] mood always
resolves bundled Koshkit utilities after \f[CR]PATH\f[R].
T}
T{
Koshka
T}@T{
\f[CR]\-\-enable\-extended\-arithmetic\f[R]
T}@T{
Always enabled in the \f[CR]kosh\f[R] mood.
T}
T{
Debug
T}@T{
\f[CR]\-\-show\-optimizer\-diagnostics\f[R]
T}@T{
Compatibility moods emit these warnings only when \f[CR]\-WW\f[R] or
\f[CR]\-WWW\f[R] is active.
T}
T{
Debug
T}@T{
\f[CR]\-N, \-\-show\-all\-exit\-codes\f[R]
T}@T{
Can be toggled at runtime with \f[CR]set \-N\f[R] and \f[CR]set +N\f[R].
T}
T{
POSIX
T}@T{
\f[CR]\-s, \-\-stdin\f[R]
T}@T{
Input processing order is specified in Invocation.
T}
T{
POSIX
T}@T{
\f[CR]\-c COMMAND, \-\-command=COMMAND\f[R]
T}@T{
May be repeated to execute multiple strings in sequence.
A syntax error exits with status \f[CR]2\f[R], an analysis error exits
with status \f[CR]1\f[R], and subsequent command strings are aborted.
T}
T{
POSIX
T}@T{
\f[CR]\-n, \-\-no\-exec\f[R]
T}@T{
Compatibility moods report only the diagnostic tiers enabled by
\f[CR]\-W\f[R].
T}
T{
POSIX
T}@T{
\f[CR]\-o NAME, +o NAME\f[R]
T}@T{
\f[CR]\-o posix\f[R] acts as \f[CR]\-\-posix\f[R].
An unknown option name exits with status \f[CR]2\f[R].
In the \f[CR]kosh\f[R] mood, \f[CR]+o nounset\f[R] is ignored and
\f[CR]+o pipefail\f[R] exits with status \f[CR]2\f[R].
T}
T{
POSIX
T}@T{
\f[CR]+a, +C, +e, +f, +n, +p, +t, +u, +v, +x\f[R]
T}@T{
\f[CR]+h\f[R] and \f[CR]+m\f[R] are accepted and ignored.
Passing \f[CR]set\f[R]\-only flags (\f[CR]\-B\f[R], \f[CR]\-E\f[R],
\f[CR]\-H\f[R], \f[CR]\-P\f[R], \f[CR]\-b\f[R], \f[CR]\-k\f[R]) on the
command line exits with status \f[CR]2\f[R].
T}
T{
POSIX
T}@T{
\f[CR]\-\-\f[R]
T}@T{
The operand immediately following \f[CR]\-\-\f[R] is treated as the
script path.
T}
T{
Bash
T}@T{
\f[CR]\-\-norc\f[R]
T}@T{
Does not affect login shells, login profiles, or \f[CR]BASH_ENV\f[R].
T}
T{
Bash
T}@T{
\f[CR]\-O NAME, +O NAME\f[R]
T}@T{
An unknown \f[CR]shopt\f[R] option exits with status \f[CR]2\f[R].
Read\-only options such as \f[CR]login_shell\f[R] are ignored.
In the \f[CR]kosh\f[R] mood, \f[CR]+O failglob\f[R] and
\f[CR]\-O nullglob\f[R] exit with status \f[CR]2\f[R].
T}
T{
Bash
T}@T{
\f[CR]\-p, \-\-privileged\f[R]
T}@T{
Skips \f[CR]ENV\f[R], \f[CR]BASH_ENV\f[R], interactive non\-login
\f[CR]rc.kosh\f[R], and all settings files, and unsets
\f[CR]KOSHCONF\f[R].
A secure execution, such as setuid or setgid, automatically drops
elevated privileges and skips all startup and settings files.
On Linux, a setuid/setgid process whose real user ID is not
\f[CR]root\f[R] also clears all capability sets (including ambient
capabilities) and aborts if privilege restoration to \f[CR]root\f[R]
remains possible.
T}
.TE
.SH CONFIGURE SHELL SETTINGS VIA BUILTINS AND VARIABLES
Shell settings are inspected and mutated via \f[CR]set\f[R],
\f[CR]shopt\f[R], and \f[CR]koshconf\f[R], and their states are exposed
through \f[CR][ \-o OPTION ]\f[R], \f[CR]SHELLOPTS\f[R], and
\f[CR]BASHOPTS\f[R].
Each setting has a permanent integer ID, a canonical \f[CR]koshconf\f[R]
dot\-separated path, a scope (\f[CR]i\f[R] for interactive
editor/history/diagnostic behavior, or \f[CR]s\f[R] for semantic script
execution behavior), and optional POSIX/Bash spellings.
.SS Namespace hierarchy and legacy aliases
.PP
.TS
