T{
Namespace prefix
T}@T{
Subsystem scope
T}@T{
Alias and mapping rules
T}
_
T{
\f[CR]mood\f[R], \f[CR]init_moods\f[R]
T}@T{
Top\-level execution and startup mood selection
T}@T{
Canonical names only.
T}
T{
\f[CR]interpreter\f[R]
T}@T{
Core shell execution semantics
T}@T{
Koshka\-only options.
Options that POSIX or Bash define carry a \f[CR]posix.\f[R] or
\f[CR]bash.\f[R] origin prefix in every namespace
(\f[CR]posix.interpreter.*\f[R], \f[CR]bash.editor.*\f[R],
\f[CR]bash.completion.*\f[R]).
An option aliases to \f[CR]posix.legacy.NAME\f[R] when POSIX
\f[CR]set \-o\f[R] accepts NAME, and to \f[CR]bash.legacy.NAME\f[R]
otherwise.
T}
T{
\f[CR]compatibility\f[R]
T}@T{
Windows path translation (\f[CR]dev_null\f[R], \f[CR]slash_tmp\f[R])
T}@T{
Koshka\-only options.
T}
T{
\f[CR]editor\f[R]
T}@T{
Line editor, \f[CR]editor.history\f[R], and \f[CR]editor.hints\f[R]
T}@T{
\f[CR]editor.base_mode\f[R] (\f[CR]emacs\f[R] or \f[CR]vi\f[R]) accepts
the aliases \f[CR]posix.legacy.vi\f[R] and \f[CR]bash.legacy.emacs\f[R].
\f[CR]set \-o emacs\f[R] and \f[CR]set \-o vi\f[R] toggle
\f[CR]editor.base_mode\f[R] as in Bash.
\f[CR]interactive_comments\f[R] aliases to
\f[CR]bash.legacy.interactive\-comments\f[R].
T}
T{
\f[CR]optimizer\f[R]
T}@T{
Static analysis tiers and source traces
T}@T{
Koshka\-only options.
T}
T{
\f[CR]completion\f[R]
T}@T{
Tab completion behavior and menu style
T}@T{
Bash programmable completion uses \f[CR]bash.completion.*\f[R].
T}
T{
\f[CR]debug\f[R]
T}@T{
AST, lexer, exit code, and memory telemetry
T}@T{
POSIX tracing options use \f[CR]posix.debug.*\f[R].
T}
.TE
.IP \[bu] 2
The first component of a \f[CR]koshconf\f[R] name is its topic.
POSIX \f[CR]set \-o\f[R] options start with \f[CR]posix\f[R]
(\f[CR]posix.interpreter.exit_on_command_failure\f[R] for
\f[CR]errexit\f[R]), other Bash \f[CR]set \-o\f[R] and \f[CR]shopt\f[R]
options start with \f[CR]bash\f[R], and every other option exists only
in Koshka.
\f[CR]editor.base_mode\f[R] is the only Bash\-derived option without a
prefix.
.IP \[bu] 2
An option with a Bash spelling is also accepted as
\f[CR]posix.legacy.NAME\f[R] when POSIX \f[CR]set \-o\f[R] accepts
\f[CR]NAME\f[R] and as \f[CR]bash.legacy.NAME\f[R] otherwise
(\f[CR]bash.legacy.extglob\f[R]).
An alias is the same option.
\f[CR]editor.base_mode\f[R] has the aliases \f[CR]posix.legacy.vi\f[R]
and \f[CR]bash.legacy.emacs\f[R].
.IP \[bu] 2
Every option operand and \f[CR]kosh.conf\f[R] entry accepts either the
canonical name or its \f[CR]posix.legacy.*\f[R] /
\f[CR]bash.legacy.*\f[R] alias.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[CR]editor.base_mode\f[R] covers the Bash options \f[CR]emacs\f[R] and
\f[CR]vi\f[R] and is \f[CR]vi\f[R] while \f[CR]set \-o vi\f[R] is on and
\f[CR]emacs\f[R] otherwise.
\f[CR]set \-o emacs\f[R], \f[CR]set \-o vi\f[R], the \f[CR]\-o\f[R]
test, and \f[CR]SHELLOPTS\f[R] behave as in Bash.
.IP \[bu] 2
Error messages, \f[CR]KOSHCONF\f[R], \f[CR]koshconf create\f[R], and
\f[CR]koshconf list\f[R] emit only canonical names.
Running \f[CR]koshconf list \-\-all\f[R] appends legacy aliases.
.IP \[bu] 2
The Bash options \f[CR]hashall\f[R] and \f[CR]nolog\f[R] have no
canonical \f[CR]koshconf\f[R] name.
They are accepted by \f[CR]set\f[R] and \f[CR]\-o\f[R], appear in
\f[CR]koshconf list \-\-all\f[R] as \f[CR]posix.legacy.hashall\f[R] and
\f[CR]posix.legacy.nolog\f[R], and are rejected by
\f[CR]koshconf get\f[R], \f[CR]koshconf set\f[R], and
\f[CR]kosh.conf\f[R].
.IP \[bu] 2
No\-op \f[CR]bash.legacy.*\f[R] \f[CR]shopt\f[R] entries (such as
\f[CR]cdspell\f[R] or \f[CR]histappend\f[R]) are accepted so legacy Bash
startup files execute without errors, but they do not alter shell
behavior.
\f[CR]shopt\f[R] accepts \f[CR]array_expand_once\f[R] as the Bash 5.3
alias for \f[CR]assoc_expand_once\f[R].
.SS Settings mutability constraints
.PP
.TS
