.TE
.SH PERSIST OPTIONS IN \f[CR]kosh.conf\f[R] SETTINGS FILES
Settings files apply globally to every shell process that loads them,
including non\-interactive scripts.
If a semantic option (\f[CR]s\f[R]) should apply only to an interactive
session rather than all scripts, set it interactively with
\f[CR]set\f[R], \f[CR]shopt\f[R], or \f[CR]koshconf set\f[R] without
\f[CR]\-\-persist\f[R].
.SS Settings file examples
Configure the \f[CR]bash\f[R] grammar with \f[CR]vi\f[R] keybindings and
a 20,000\-entry history limit.
.IP
.EX
mood=bash
editor.history.max_entries=20000
editor.base_mode=vi
.EE
.PP
Keep the strict \f[CR]kosh\f[R] execution mood while sourcing
\f[CR]\[ti]/.bashrc\f[R] under the \f[CR]bash\f[R] mood at interactive
startup.
.IP
.EX
init_moods=bash
.EE
.PP
Run \f[CR]koshconf set <option> <value> \-\-persist\f[R] to write these
entries programmatically.
.SS File paths, security checks, and write semantics
.PP
.TS
tab(@);
l l l.
T{
Tier
T}@T{
File path
T}@T{
Ownership, permissions, and write behavior
T}
_
T{
System
T}@T{
\f[CR]/etc/kosh/kosh.conf\f[R] (POSIX) or
\f[CR]%ProgramData%\[rs]kosh\[rs]kosh.conf\f[R] (Windows)
T}@T{
Loaded first, and only when no other user can write it.
On POSIX, the file and its parent directory must be owned by
\f[CR]root\f[R] and not writable by group or others.
On Windows, the file and its \f[CR]kosh\f[R] directory must be owned by
\f[CR]SYSTEM\f[R], \f[CR]Administrators\f[R], or
\f[CR]TrustedInstaller\f[R], and no ACL entry may grant another account
permission to modify, change permissions, or take ownership (the
directory may allow others to add files).
If the check fails, the file is skipped with a warning.
T}
T{
User
T}@T{
\f[CR]$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/kosh/kosh.conf\f[R] or
\f[CR]\[ti]/.config/kosh/kosh.conf\f[R]
T}@T{
Loaded second.
\f[CR]XDG_CONFIG_HOME\f[R] is used only when set to an absolute path; on
Windows, \f[CR]\[ti]\f[R] falls back to \f[CR]USERPROFILE\f[R] when
\f[CR]HOME\f[R] is unset.
On POSIX, a shell running with an effective user ID of \f[CR]root\f[R]
neither reads nor writes this file.
Written by \f[CR]koshconf create\f[R],
\f[CR]koshconf set \-\-persist\f[R], and
\f[CR]koshconf save \-\-persist\f[R].
Writing preserves symlinks (updating the target), preserves existing
file permissions (or applies \f[CR]0644\f[R] minus \f[CR]umask\f[R] for
new files), creates missing parent directories, and backs up the prior
file to \f[CR]kosh.conf.bak\f[R].
T}
T{
System rc script
T}@T{
\f[CR]/etc/kosh/rc.kosh\f[R] (POSIX) or
\f[CR]%ProgramData%\[rs]kosh\[rs]rc.kosh\f[R] (Windows)
T}@T{
Sourced by a login or interactive \f[CR]kosh\f[R] mood before the user
rc script (see Startup script).
It passes the same ownership and permission checks as the system
settings file and is skipped with a warning when they fail.
T}
T{
User rc script
T}@T{
\f[CR]$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/kosh/scripts/rc.kosh\f[R] or
\f[CR]\[ti]/.config/kosh/scripts/rc.kosh\f[R]
T}@T{
Sourced by a login or interactive \f[CR]kosh\f[R] mood (see Startup
script); skipped by the POSIX superuser.
Startup files of the other moods are listed under Bash and POSIX startup
files.
T}
.TE
.SS File grammar and serialization rules
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Line structure\f[R] \- Lines terminate with \f[CR]LF\f[R] or
\f[CR]CRLF\f[R], and a leading UTF\-8 BOM is ignored.
Blank lines and lines beginning with \f[CR]#\f[R] (after optional
whitespace) are ignored.
Active lines follow the format \f[CR]name=value\f[R], ignoring
surrounding whitespace around \f[CR]name\f[R] and \f[CR]value\f[R].
Values cannot span multiple lines.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Quoting\f[R] \- A value beginning with \f[CR]\[aq]\f[R] or
\f[CR]\[dq]\f[R] terminates at the matching closing quote, and only
whitespace may follow the closing quote on that line.
Surround a value with quotes to preserve leading or trailing whitespace.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Data types\f[R] \- Booleans accept \f[CR]on\f[R], \f[CR]off\f[R],
\f[CR]true\f[R], \f[CR]false\f[R], \f[CR]1\f[R], or \f[CR]0\f[R].
Enumerations take their literal string identifier.
Strings must be valid UTF\-8 without \f[CR]NUL\f[R] bytes.
Integers are \f[CR]editor.history.max_entries\f[R] (\f[CR]0\f[R] to
\f[CR]2147483647\f[R]) and \f[CR]editor.wrap_width\f[R] (\f[CR]0\f[R] to
\f[CR]65535\f[R]).
\f[CR]init_moods\f[R] takes a comma\-separated list of moods without
empty elements.
A name may be the canonical name or its legacy alias.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Evaluation order and mood locks\f[R] \- The \f[CR]mood\f[R]
directive is evaluated first regardless of line position, and the other
lines are read under the resulting session mood.
When \f[CR]mood=kosh\f[R] is active, any line that attempts to change an
option locked by the \f[CR]kosh\f[R] mood (see Moods) is skipped with a
warning.
Later lines overwrite earlier lines, across the system file, the user
file, and \f[CR]KOSHCONF\f[R] in that order.
A settings file may set every option except the read\-only,
command\-line fixed, and session or mood derived options listed under
Shell settings.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]\f[CB]koshconf\f[B] serialization\f[R] \- When writing
\f[CR]kosh.conf\f[R], \f[CR]koshconf\f[R] quotes values that begin with
a quote or have leading/trailing whitespace, and preserves
\f[CR]CRLF\f[R] line endings if already present.
A value containing a newline, or requiring quotes while containing both
\f[CR]\[aq]\f[R] and \f[CR]\[dq]\f[R], cannot be serialized;
\f[CR]koshconf set \-\-persist\f[R] rejects the operation before
mutating session state.
Persisting \f[CR]mood=kosh\f[R] strips existing lines for options locked
by the \f[CR]kosh\f[R] mood.
Unrecognized option lines from newer or custom versions are preserved at
the bottom of the rewritten file.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Template generation (\f[CB]koshconf create\f[B])\f[R] \- Writes all
file\-permitted options, each documented with a comment and preset
value.
Only \f[CR]mood\f[R] is uncommented; all other options remain commented
out so system defaults apply until explicitly overridden.
String options without defaults are written as commented\-out empty
assignments.
The \f[CR]kosh\f[R] preset populates the exact values locked by the
\f[CR]kosh\f[R] mood so uncommenting them triggers no warnings.
.SS Settings file error handling
A settings file error never aborts shell startup.
.PP
.TS
tab(@);
l l.
T{
Failure condition
T}@T{
Shell recovery behavior
T}
_
T{
File does not exist
T}@T{
Silently skipped.
T}
T{
File is unreadable, exceeds \f[CR]1 MiB\f[R], is not a regular file, or
fails system ownership/ACL validation
T}@T{
Emits a warning and skips the entire file.
T}
T{
Line lacks \f[CR]=\f[R], specifies an unknown or disallowed option,
contains an invalid value, has an unclosed quote, or has trailing
non\-whitespace characters after a closing quote
T}@T{
Emits a warning with the file path and line number, and skips that line.
T}
.TE
.SH EXECUTE STARTUP LOGIC IN \f[CR]rc.kosh\f[R]
An interactive or login shell running in the \f[CR]kosh\f[R] mood
sources the system \f[CR]rc.kosh\f[R] and then the user
\f[CR]rc.kosh\f[R] after loading settings files.
The system script is \f[CR]/etc/kosh/rc.kosh\f[R] (or
\f[CR]%ProgramData%\[rs]kosh\[rs]rc.kosh\f[R]), runs whenever it exists
and passes the checks of the system settings file, and runs for the
POSIX superuser too.
The user script is \f[CR]$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/kosh/scripts/rc.kosh\f[R] (or
\f[CR]\[ti]/.config/kosh/scripts/rc.kosh\f[R]) and runs second, so its
commands can override those of the system script.
Koshka does not read \f[CR]/etc/koshrc\f[R] or \f[CR]\[ti]/.koshrc\f[R].
.PP
The \f[CR]kosh\f[R] mood does not distinguish login and non\-login
shells when it selects startup scripts, because that distinction is
obsolete.
A script that still needs it can test \f[CR]shopt \-q login_shell\f[R]
to run login\-only commands.
Place procedural shell code in \f[CR]rc.kosh\f[R] and declarative
options in \f[CR]kosh.conf\f[R].
See Startup files for invocation flags that replace or skip
\f[CR]rc.kosh\f[R], and Bash and POSIX startup files for compatibility
mood startup paths.
.PP
When \f[CR]rc.kosh\f[R] is missing, \f[CR]koshconf create\f[R] generates
the following default script (existing files are never overwritten).
.IP
.EX
\f[I]#!/usr/bin/env kosh\f[R]
\f[B]if\f[R] shopt \-q login_shell\f[B];\f[R] \f[B]then\f[R]
  [ \-r /etc/profile ] \f[B]&&\f[R] . /etc/profile
  \f[B]if\f[R] [ \-r \[ti]/.bash_profile ]\f[B];\f[R] \f[B]then\f[R]
    . \[ti]/.bash_profile
  \f[B]elif\f[R] [ \-r \[ti]/.bash_login ]\f[B];\f[R] \f[B]then\f[R]
    . \[ti]/.bash_login
  \f[B]elif\f[R] [ \-r \[ti]/.profile ]\f[B];\f[R] \f[B]then\f[R]
    . \[ti]/.profile
  \f[B]fi\f[R]
\f[B]else\f[R]
  [ \-r \[ti]/.profile ] \f[B]&&\f[R] . \[ti]/.profile
  [ \-r \[ti]/.bashrc ] \f[B]&&\f[R] . \[ti]/.bashrc
\f[B]fi\f[R]
.EE
.SH PROPAGATE INTERACTIVE SETTINGS WITH \f[CR]KOSHCONF\f[R]
\f[CR]KOSHCONF\f[R] is a dynamic shell variable that serializes the
active \f[CR]mood\f[R] and interactive (\f[CR]i\f[R]) options into a
padded standard Base64 string.
Direct user assignments to \f[CR]KOSHCONF\f[R] are ignored; reading or
exporting \f[CR]KOSHCONF\f[R] dynamically generates the current session
payload.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Excluded options\f[R] \- Semantic (\f[CR]s\f[R]) options (other
than \f[CR]mood\f[R]), \f[CR]init_moods\f[R],
\f[CR]editor.history.file_path\f[R],
\f[CR]posix.interpreter.job_control\f[R],
\f[CR]bash.editor.history.bang_expansion\f[R],
\f[CR]bash.editor.history.records_commands\f[R], and
\f[CR]bash.interpreter.login_shell\f[R] are never serialized into
\f[CR]KOSHCONF\f[R].
Child shells load semantic options directly from settings files.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Export and ingestion\f[R] \- \f[CR]KOSHCONF\f[R] is not exported by
default.
After \f[CR]export KOSHCONF\f[R], or when passed as a command prefix
(\f[CR]KOSHCONF=x cmd\f[R] evaluates to the current dynamic payload),
each started command receives the settings current at its start.
A starting \f[CR]kosh\f[R] process applies \f[CR]KOSHCONF\f[R] after
reading settings files and removes \f[CR]KOSHCONF\f[R] from the
environment before running commands.
An active shell can ingest a payload at runtime via
\f[CR]koshconf load\f[R].
.PP
Forward local interactive settings to a remote SSH session by passing
\f[CR]KOSHCONF\f[R] on the remote command line.
.IP
.EX
ssh host KOSHCONF=\[dq]$KOSHCONF\[dq] kosh
.EE
.SS Binary wire format
The option ids are listed under Shell settings and never change meaning.
Base64 decoding yields a contiguous sequence of binary records in the
format \f[CR][option_id][value_length][value_bytes]\f[R].
.PP
.TS
tab(@);
l l.
T{
Field or data type
T}@T{
Wire representation
T}
_
T{
\f[CR]option_id\f[R] and \f[CR]value_length\f[R]
T}@T{
Unsigned LEB128 varints (7 data bits per byte, least significant group
first, high continuation bit \f[CR]0x80\f[R] set on all bytes except the
final byte).
T}
T{
Boolean
T}@T{
\f[CR]1\f[R] byte (\f[CR]0x00\f[R] for off, \f[CR]0x01\f[R] for on).
T}
T{
Enumeration
T}@T{
Unsigned LEB128 varint representing the zero\-based index of the value
in its canonical list.
T}
T{
Integer
T}@T{
ASCII decimal digit bytes.
T}
T{
String
T}@T{
Raw UTF\-8 bytes.
T}
.TE
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Canonical validation\f[R] \- The decoder enforces strict canonical
encoding.
Base64 padding (\f[CR]=\f[R]) must be present, every LEB128 varint must
use the minimum number of bytes, and \f[CR]option_id\f[R] values must
appear in strictly ascending numeric order.
.IP \[bu] 2
\f[B]Decode error recovery\f[R] \- If a record contains an unknown
\f[CR]option_id\f[R], an option excluded from \f[CR]KOSHCONF\f[R], or a
value invalid in \f[CR]kosh.conf\f[R], that individual record is skipped
with a warning.
If the payload violates Base64 padding, minimal varint encoding,
ascending ID order, or record length boundaries (truncation), the entire
\f[CR]KOSHCONF\f[R] payload is rejected with a warning.
